Given an array of integers nums and an integer k, return the total number of subarrays whose sum equals to k.

A subarray is a contiguous non-empty sequence of elements within an array.

 

Example 1:

Input: nums = [1,1,1], k = 2
Output: 2
Example 2:

Input: nums = [1,2,3], k = 3
Output: 2
 

public static int subarraySumEqualsK(int[] input, int K){

    // Edge case: if the array is null or empty,
    // there are no subarrays to check.
    if(input == null || input.length == 0){
        return -1;
    }

    // Stores the total number of subarrays
    // whose sum equals K.
    int count = 0;

    // Running (prefix) sum from index 0 to current index.
    int sum = 0;

    // HashMap:
    // Key   = Prefix Sum
    // Value = Number of times this prefix sum has occurred.
    Map<Integer, Integer> map = new HashMap<Integer, Integer>();

    // Insert prefix sum 0 once before processing the array.
    // This handles cases where a subarray starting at index 0
    // itself sums to K.
    map.put(0, 1);

    // Traverse every element in the array.
    for(int i = 0; i < input.length; i++){

        // Update the running prefix sum.
        sum += input[i];

        // Check if there was a previous prefix sum equal to (sum - K).
        // If yes, then the subarray between that previous position
        // and the current index sums to K.
        if(map.containsKey(sum - K)){

            // Add the number of times (sum - K) has occurred.
            // There may be multiple valid subarrays ending at this index.
            count += map.get(sum - K);
        }

        // Record the current prefix sum in the HashMap.
        // If it already exists, increment its frequency.
        map.put(sum, map.getOrDefault(sum, 0) + 1);
    }

    // Return the total number of subarrays whose sum equals K.
    return count;
}
